  "about": "The metro areas the app covers. Each area is a list of counties (5-digit FIPS codes; Connecticut uses its planning regions, which replaced its counties in 2022), grouped into the regions the title bar names. \"timeZone\" is the area's own time zone (an IANA name): the app shows the area's times in it, and this is the one place the app names a time zone. The counties' outlines are in area_counties.bin, made by tools/generate_areas.py from the US Census Bureau's 2023 cartographic boundary file. \"states\" names the states the areas' counties are in, by the first two digits of a county's code: the app's full list of areas says which states each area is in, and its search finds an area by them; a state's name is translated like the other text (CatchText). \"outside\" lists the counties in no area that the service area's boxes (ServiceArea.boxes) reach into: their outlines are in area_counties.bin too, so the app knows a place there is outside the service area, and generate_areas.py checks that the list is complete. \"operators\" says, for every operator, the metro areas it has stops in and the kinds of vehicles it runs in each (the ids: a FerryFeed.Source, \"bus:\" and a RegionalBusFeed code, or one of the operators that ship inside the app: subway, sir, tram, mtaBus, sle), and \"credit\" is who the data is credited to in the station footer (a bus company: its own name). \"short\" is a shorter name for one whose credit or own name is long, where a few words name whose live times are shown (the menu bar's and the widget's \"MBTA live\"); without it the credit is used. NJ Transit's light rail lines are each in the areas they stop in (\"lines\").",
